(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-disorders-in-immunity-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Disorders in Immunity — Quiz 2","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is hemolytic disease of the newborn prevented?","choices":["Passive immunization with anti-Rh antibody","Active immunization of the mother against Rh","Transfusing the mother with Rh positive blood","Cross-matching the fetus against the mother"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — Rhogam prevents sensitization of the mother, which is why it must be given before her own response can develop. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What do basophils initiate?","choices":["Complement activation by the classical pathway","Cytotoxic T cell proliferation","A TH2 response and production of IgE","A TH1 response and production of IgG"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are rare granulocytes, and in normal defense they act against helminths — the same axis that produces allergy when misdirected. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Some tumor cells cleave a stress molecule that natural killer cells recognize and release it in soluble form. What is the effect?","choices":["Natural killer cells destroy it faster","Complement lyses the tumor cell","Mast cells degranulate at the tumor","The tumor cell escapes being killed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The soluble molecule binds the killing receptor on natural killer and gamma-delta T cells, so the variant tumor cell is not killed and proliferates. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How do corticosteroids act in allergy?","choices":["They inhibit lymphocytes to reduce IgE","They block mast cell degranulation","They bind histamine receptors on target organs","They block leukotriene synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, acting upstream on the production of the antibody rather than on the mediators it releases. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the acute response in allergic asthma?","choices":["Cytokine and eosinophil granule mediated inflammation","Immune complex deposition in the alveolar membrane","Complement-mediated lysis of airway epithelium","Bronchial smooth muscle contraction with increased mucus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, producing airway obstruction. It is triggered by inhaled allergen crosslinking specific IgE on mast cell surfaces. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"An antibody against CD20 is conjugated to a radionuclide. How does it kill malignant B cells?","choices":["It removes the T cell off signal","It replicates inside the tumor","Radiation damages their genome","It blocks their mitotic spindle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The radioactive antibody binds the malignant B cells and irradiates them, and the radiation damages their deoxyribonucleic acid and kills them. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the current status of tumor infiltrating lymphocyte therapy?","choices":["Abandoned after the 1890s","In development, not yet approved","Approved for different blood cancers","Approved for metastatic melanoma only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tumor infiltrating lymphocytes, cytotoxic T cells expanded to a therapeutic amount, are still in development and not yet approved. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What is omalizumab?","choices":["A monoclonal antibody against IgE","A leukotriene synthesis blocker","A histamine receptor blocker","A mast cell stabilizer"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so it removes the antibody that arms the mast cell rather than acting on the cell or its mediators. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which hypersensitivity type is chronic asthma?","choices":["Type III","Type IV","Type I","Type II"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, mediated by cytokines and eosinophil granules — so a single disease can begin as type I and become type IV as it becomes chronic. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why is cancer described as both a cause and an effect of immune dysfunction?","choices":["Immune failure permits it; the tumor then suppresses immunity","Cancer cells are destroyed only by antibody","Cancer only arises in people with no immune system","Cancer is classified as an overreaction of immunity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immune surveillance normally keeps cancer in check, and successful tumors evade and actively manipulate the response once established. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does chronic rejection cause a graft to fail?","choices":["Thickened vessel walls starve it","Preexisting antibodies cause hemorrhage","Donor T cells attack the recipient's skin","Immunoglobulin E triggers mast cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immune complexes in the vessel walls recruit inflammatory cells, the walls thicken, and the graft's blood supply becomes inadequate. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"In acute rejection of a kidney, where do the graft's dendritic cells go to activate effector T cells?","choices":["The renal pelvis","The spleen","The thymus","The bone marrow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Dendritic cells carried in the graft migrate to the spleen, activate effector T cells there, and those T cells return via the blood to destroy the graft. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient receives a heart valve taken from a pig. Which graft type is this?","choices":["An allograft","An isograft","An autograft","A xenograft"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A xenograft is tissue from another species, here a pig transplanted into a human. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is said about the relationship between the portal of entry and the organ affected?","choices":["They are always the same","They are never the same","The portal determines the severity but not the site","They may or may not be the same"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why an ingested food allergen can produce urticaria in the skin — antigen entering blood vessels is carried there. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Graft success depends mainly on compatibility of which molecules?","choices":["Toll-like pattern receptors","Histocompatibility antigens","Complement regulatory proteins","Immunoglobulin E receptors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rejection is associated with the major histocompatibility complex (MHC), and different grafts succeed to different degrees because of major histocompatibility complex compatibility. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Autoantibodies bind receptors on thyroid follicle cells and raise thyroxine levels. Which disease is this?","choices":["Multiple sclerosis","Graves' disease","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Myasthenia gravis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In Graves' disease autoantibodies attach to receptors on the follicle cells that secrete thyroxine, increasing it and causing hyperthyroidism. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What is familial about allergy?","choices":["The route by which allergens enter","Nothing — allergy has no familial component","A generalized predisposition","The specific allergen a person reacts to"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — families inherit the tendency rather than the particular allergen, which is why relatives may react to entirely different things. The specific allergy is not inherited. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is a secondary immunodeficiency?","choices":["Congenital agammaglobulinemia","Severe combined immunodeficiency","Suppression after chemotherapy","DiGeorge syndrome from birth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chemotherapy, radiation, infection such as the human immunodeficiency virus, and blood cell cancers cause secondary immunodeficiency after birth. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors are currently used for which cancers?","choices":["Melanoma and lung cancer","Bladder cancer only","Prostate cancer only","Blood cancers only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Some checkpoint inhibitors are currently used for melanoma and some lung cancers. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is required for a type III reaction to occur?","choices":["Prior sensitization with IgE","A T cell mediated memory response","Only a trace of antigen","A large amount of antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is part of why serum sickness follows exposure to foreign animal proteins in quantity rather than to a trace allergen. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"About what share of bone marrow recipients develop graft versus host disease?","choices":["About 90%","About 30%","About 3%","About 60%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Graft versus host disease affects about 30% of marrow recipients, reflecting marrow's sensitivity to human leukocyte antigen discrepancies. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Adenosine deaminase deficiency causes which kind of immunodeficiency?","choices":["Severe combined immunodeficiency","A secondary immunodeficiency","An isolated complement defect","An autoimmune thyroid disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine deaminase deficiency causes some types of severe combined immunodeficiency, a congenital, primary defect. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What does cromolyn sodium do?","choices":["Reverses airway constriction directly","Blocks degranulation","Reduces IgE production by inhibiting lymphocytes","Binds to IgE as a monoclonal antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it prevents the mast cell from releasing its granule contents rather than blocking the mediators afterwards. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which exposures can activate oncogenes?","choices":["Horse serum, animal drugs or hormones","Metals, cosmetics or poison ivy","Radiation, chemicals or viruses","Pollen, dust mites or animal dander"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oncogenes may be activated by exposure to radiation, chemicals or oncogenic viruses during transformation. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the main challenge of oncolytic viral therapy?","choices":["No virus is naturally oncolytic","Immunity may clear the virus first","The virus destroys normal cells","The virus cannot infect tumor cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The immune system may wipe out the oncolytic virus before it does its job, and only a few are approved, just one in the United States, for melanoma. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"From what does a cancer arise?","choices":["One cell with a single mutation","Immune cells that lose tolerance","One cell with multiple mutations","Many cells with one shared mutation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cancer arises from a single cell that has accumulated multiple mutations, with oncogenes activated by radiation, chemicals or oncogenic viruses. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"When does an Rh negative person produce anti-Rh antibodies?","choices":["Whenever they encounter any foreign protein","Only if they are also ABO incompatible","Only after sensitization","From birth, without any exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — unlike ABO, where antibodies against the antigens you lack are already present, Rh requires prior exposure. A bad transfusion or an incompatible pregnancy does it. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Where are tumor specific antigens found?","choices":["On tumor cells only","On tumor and normal cells alike","Only on normal cells near a tumor","Only on red blood cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tumor specific antigens are present on tumor cells but not on normal cells, derived from viral proteins or mutated cellular proteins. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How is allergy or hypersensitivity defined?","choices":["A response using mechanisms unique to allergic disease","An abnormal response directed against self antigens","A loss or deficiency of immune function","An exaggerated, misdirected immune response to an allergen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the critical point is that it uses the SAME mechanisms as protective immune reactions — nothing novel is happening, it is simply happening against the wrong target. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Autoimmunity involves which hypersensitivity types?","choices":["Type I only","All four types","Type IV only","All except type I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Autoimmunity involves types II, III and IV, but not type I, the immunoglobulin E mediated allergy. — Lecture 7  Dr. Webster  Disorders in Immunity.pptx, Slide 52"}]};
